# Day 8 – Reflection

## What I Learned

Day 8 helped me understand how Salesforce can handle work asynchronously instead of making users wait for every operation to finish.

I learned the purpose and basic usage of:

- Future Methods
- Queueable Apex
- Batch Apex
- Scheduled Apex

## Practical Experience

I created and tested a Queueable Apex job for post-offer processing.

I also created a Future Method to demonstrate asynchronous Application processing.

For large datasets, I implemented Batch Apex and understood its Start, Execute, and Finish lifecycle.

I also created Scheduled Apex to identify expired Job records and scheduled it to run at a specific time.

## What I Improved

I improved my understanding of how to choose an asynchronous solution based on the business requirement.

I also learned how to monitor asynchronous jobs using Salesforce tools such as `AsyncApexJob` and `CronTrigger`.

## Key Takeaway

The most important lesson from Day 8 was that asynchronous Apex is not simply about running code in the background.

The correct approach depends on the workload:

- Work that can happen later → Future or Queueable
- Structured background work → Queueable
- Large datasets → Batch Apex
- Time-based work → Scheduled Apex

## Overall Reflection

Day 8 gave me practical experience with asynchronous Apex and helped me understand how these technologies can be used in a real Placement Management System.

I also learned that good Salesforce development requires choosing the right technology based on the business requirement rather than using a particular feature just because it is available.
